@prefix pm:      <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/ontology#> .
@prefix pmc:     <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/concepts/> .
@prefix rdfs:    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix skos:    <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#> .
@prefix dcterms: <http://purl.org/dc/terms/> .

#
# prima-materia — where the two paths are said to part
#
# A dispute and not a verdict. Both sides are read in their own works, both
# use the same term for the same thing, and they value it oppositely. The node
# exists because a record that reported only one of them would settle by
# whichever book it happened to open.
#

pmc:WhetherTheSelfIsKeptOrSurrendered a pm:Disputing ;
    rdfs:label "Whether keeping the self is the goal of the path or its failure"@en, "Ob das Bewahren des Selbst das Ziel des Weges ist oder sein Scheitern"@de ;
    skos:definition "A disagreement about what becomes of the bounded self at the end of the path, carried on inside the modern occult literature with the same term on both sides. One party holds that the self which is not surrendered ossifies and is consumed, and calls that the second death; the other holds that a self brought forth and made to endure is the attainment, and names it with a word of self-generation."@en ;
    skos:note "The sides do not differ over means, and both say so. The one states that the signpost of a right-hand and a left-hand path is barely legible, that the two look exactly alike for a long way, and that the question is at which point they diverge; he then puts the divergence at the crossing of the Abyss, and separates the sorcerer from the brother of the left-hand path as sharply as a thief from a tyrant. The other derives his word from an Egyptian term for coming into being and applies it to the transformation of the will, not to any particular practice. Whatever is at issue between them, it is not method."@en ;
    skos:note "That is why the dispute is worth holding as a node. Read as a quarrel about practices it dissolves into nothing, because neither side defines the path by practices. Read as a quarrel about the end state it is sharp and undecidable from inside either work, because each is describing the same movement and calling it by the opposite name. See prima-materia#61 and prima-materia#68."@en ;
    skos:note "One asymmetry the record should not smooth. The earlier of the two sides values the matter from a unitive end and treats the surviving self as a remainder that time consumes; the later values it from a separative end and treats the same survival as the work itself. Neither is a misreading of the other. A continuity story that runs from the one to the other as a single tradition with a changing mood reports a break as a development."@en ;
    pm:disputedClaim pmc:XeperAsSelfGeneration ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    dcterms:source "A. Crowley, Magick Without Tears, chapter XII, on the left-hand path and the black brothers (electronic text of the collected letters)", "M. A. Aquino, The Temple of Set (Temple of Set, San Francisco), in the account of the Word Xeper and its Egyptian derivation" .
